        "id": "p_ch1",
        "entry": "Banking Customer Churn \u2014 propensity model",
        "score": 7.33,
        "keywords": 4.0,
        "domain": 3.33,
        "chosen": false,
        "text": "Predicted bank-customer churn with a supervised XGBoost propensity classifier in R (tidymodels): two-stage Bayesian hyperparameter tuning, SMOTE inside the preprocessing recipe and MCC evaluation."
      },
      {
        "id": "p_gg5",
        "entry": "GTAI Cell Line Finder \u2014 multi-omics graph for cancer cell-line selection (PoC)",
        "score": 6.0,
        "keywords": 4.0,
        "domain": 2.0,
        "chosen": true,
        "text": "Integrated 14 public omics source files into one graph of 1,399 cell lines and 16,385 genes, cut RAM from 8.2 to 5.5 GB on a free T4 GPU, reported the weak results openly (train Pearson 0.807 vs test 0.545) and debugged a hash-randomisation bug to its root cause."
      },
      {
        "id": "p_cn1",
        "entry": "Image Classification \u2014 CNN and Transfer Learning",
        "score": 6.0,
        "keywords": 6.0,
        "domain": 0.0,
        "chosen": false,
        "text": "Trained a PyTorch CNN on CIFAR-10 and compared it with ImageNet pretrained ResNet50 transfer learning (frozen backbone, new 10 class head), judged by confusion matrices and validation loss rather than accuracy alone."
      },
      {
        "id": "az1",
        "entry": "AstraZeneca",
        "score": 4.0,
        "keywords": 2.0,
        "domain": 2.0,
        "chosen": false,
        "text": "Built a tool-calling LLM agent (LangChain, AWS Bedrock, Claude Haiku 4.5) for a multi-omics cancer research platform, with three tools for gene alias resolution, scoring-methodology explanation and dataset provenance."
      },
      {
        "id": "az9",
        "entry": "AstraZeneca",
        "score": 4.0,
        "keywords": 4.0,
        "domain": 0.0,
        "chosen": true,
        "text": "Wrote the dissertation's AI-agent sections covering architecture, implementation and evaluation, including a documented hallucination-rate analysis and test-case outcomes."
      },
      {
        "id": "az10",
        "entry": "AstraZeneca",
        "score": 4.0,
        "keywords": 2.0,
        "domain": 2.0,
        "chosen": true,
        "text": "Needed an agent that researchers could trust on a cancer research platform, including when its tools fail: built a tool-calling agent (LangChain, Claude Haiku 4.5 on AWS Bedrock) for three use cases (gene alias resolution, prompt-engineered explanations of a fixed scoring pipeline, validated dataset descriptions), with deterministic Ensembl and HGNC calls, deterministic hallucination guards, and circuit breakers with a 19,213-gene parquet fallback for when the external APIs are unavailable."
      },
      {
